  "query_preview": "..The short term weather through Friday will be fairly \nbenign across southeast Arizona as broad ridging aloft will \ngenerally prevail. There is still quite a bit of lower level \nmoisture out there and that is reflected well in the surface \ndewpoints that remain in the 50s. A weak disturbance aloft is \npushing south towards the White Mountains and has generated a couple \nof showers and thunderstorms up that way this afternoon with the \nrest of the area remaining dry. With the modestly elevated lower to \nmid level moisture, we'll see a few build-ups again Thursday and \nFriday mainly in the Sky Islands but have kept any mentionable \nprecipitation limited to the White Mountains. Otherwise, we'll \ncontinue to see above normal temperatures through Friday with some \neasterly breezes kicking in starting Thursday. A few of the normally \nsusceptible east wind locales will have gusts up to around 20-25 mph \non Friday. \n\nThe main weather focus will be this weekend into next week as the \nactive eastern Pacific hurricane season will again have an influence \non our weather. In the broader scale, a trough off the California \ncoast will result in an abrupt recurvature of Hurricane Rachel \ntowards the far northern part of Baja California. The latest NHC \nforecast has Rachel as a post-tropical system as it approaches far \nnorthern Baja Sunday morning, with the remnants moving up the lower \nColorado River Valley during the day Sunday. Moisture levels will be \non the rise for us Saturday with PWATs increasing into the 1 to 1.25 \ninch range. Expect scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop on \nSaturday afternoon continuing into Saturday evening. The latest \ntrajectory of Rachel would keep the bulk of the precipitation along \nthe immediate path of the system and mostly to the west of the \nforecast area on Sunday, with any shower and thunderstorm activity \nover most of our area more scattered in nature. Meanwhile, breezy to \nwindy conditions are expected for area on Sunday with sustained \nsouth to southwest winds of 15-30 MPH with gusts around 40 MPH.  \n\nThereafter, we'll still have the longwave trough and upper level low \nto our west through Tuesday and we also need to keep an eye on \npotential moisture from T.D. Twenty-E now off the southern coast of \nMexico as it pushes to the north. Most of the guidance keeps any \nsignificant impacts to our east in New Mexico late Monday into \nTuesday but there is a potential that can adjust either to the east \nor west. Otherwise, given the south to southwest flow ahead of the \ntrough, shower and thunderstorm chances will continue both Monday \nand Tuesday. Monday will still be breezy as well, but not as strong \nas Sunday. \n\nQPF amounts for the Saturday through Tuesday time period still look \nmostly in the 0.25\" to 0.75\" range in the valleys with 1 to 2 inches \nin the Sky Islands and White Mountains. However, this is over a \nlonger duration with shorter 24 hour QPF amounts mostly less than \n0.25\" each day. Keep in mind we are talking days 4 through 7 here so \nthis can obviously adjust but for the most part, at least for our \nforecast area from a precipitation perspective this isn't looking \nlike a high impact event at this time. The most notable thing for \nthis event will be the prolonged period of unsettled weather, with \nmuch cooler temperatures and those gusty winds especially on Sunday.",
